 ],
 "summary_html": "<p>The <b>domes of the Early Middle Ages</b> built by Arab Muslim and Western European Christian states, particularly in those areas recently under Byzantine control, were an extension of earlier Roman architecture. The early Islamic domes in the Syrian region as well as the Mausoleum of Theodoric in Italy are believed to have benefited from the work of Syrian craftsmen. The Dome of the Rock, an Umayyad Muslim religious shrine built in Jerusalem, was designed similarly to nearby Byzantine martyria and Christian churches. Domes were also built as part of Muslim palaces, throne halls, pavilions, and baths, and blended elements of both Byzantine and Persian architecture, using both pendentives and squinches. Domes were included as part of Muslim palaces prior to their use in mosques, where early domes were likely used to mark the place of the ruler during ceremonies.</p>",
 "summary_text": "The domes of the Early Middle Ages built by Arab Muslim and Western European Christian states, particularly in those areas recently under Byzantine control, were an extension of earlier Roman architecture. The early Islamic domes in the Syrian region as well as the Mausoleum of Theodoric in Italy are believed to have benefited from the work of Syrian craftsmen. The Dome of the Rock, an Umayyad Muslim religious shrine built in Jerusalem, was designed similarly to nearby Byzantine martyria and Christian churches. Domes were also built as part of Muslim palaces, throne halls, pavilions, and baths, and blended elements of both Byzantine and Persian architecture, using both pendentives and squinches. Domes were included as part of Muslim palaces prior to their use in mosques, where early domes were likely used to mark the place of the ruler during ceremonies.",
